hexagon: htp: move each Q8_0 column tile of the HVX matmul with one DMA row

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The cost

The HVX path of the Q8_0 matmul (the decode, 1 to 4 activation rows) moves the weights of each
32-row column tile from DDR to VTCM with one 2D DMA descriptor: n_k rows of 1088 bytes (one weight
tile in each row), at a pitch of 1152 bytes in VTCM. The DMA engine divides a row of 1088 bytes
into five bus requests, and the last request has 64 bytes. The DMA probe of the phone stage bw
(tools/ddrbw, HTP0 v79, 6 threads) reads rows of 1088 bytes at 54.4 GB/s, and one row of the same
87040 bytes at 62.3 GB/s. The DDR ceiling is 61 to 63 GB/s. A decode token of the 4B Q8_0 at a
depth of 4096 uses 85.2 ms for its weight matmuls, at 52.7 GB/s.

The change

The tiles of a column tile are adjacent in DDR. hvx_mm_push_weight_tiles (matmul-ops.c) moves
them with one descriptor row when the VTCM pitch is the tile size. The Q8_0 dots of the tiled
activation read the tiles at a pitch of 1088 bytes, the packed layout (hvx-mm-kernels-tiled.h):
  - 1088 bytes are 8.5 vectors, thus an even tile starts on a vector edge and an odd tile starts
    64 bytes after one. The dots load only full vectors from vector edges.
  - A shuffle of 64-byte elements puts the lower halves of two weight groups into one vector and
    their upper halves into a second vector. A shuffle of 2-byte elements of the two vectors then
    gives the two groups. The dots of the checkout use a rotation and a shuffle for each group. The
    32x1 dot and the multi-row dots first do a valign of each vector by the offset of the tile (0 or
    64 bytes), thus one loop step serves the even and the odd tiles. The 32x2 dot does one more
    64-byte shuffle for an odd tile.
  - The 32x1 dot and the multi-row dots do one k-tile in each loop step. The 32x2 dot does an even
    and an odd k-tile in each step.
  - Each dot computes the same weight vectors and the same arithmetic in the same order. The
    multi-row dots keep one k-tile in each loop step: with two k-tiles in one step the compiler
    gives the qf32 result of the first tile to the add of the second tile without the conversion to
    IEEE. A qf32 result is not always equal to its IEEE value. Thus with two k-tiles in one step the
    v73 and v75 libraries in the simulator, and the v79 library on the phone (the stage gemv-check),
    gave other bits than the checkout for 4 rows, although the v79 simulator gave the same bits. In
    the v79 library of this patch, each multi-row dot has the qf32 instruction forms of the checkout
    (a vmpy, a vadd and four conversions to IEEE for each tile and row), and no qf32 add of two qf32
    values.
The Q8_0 dots get new names (tiled_vec_dot_q8_0_packed_32x1 and the others), thus a caller that
moves the tiles at the old pitch does not compile.

The paths that change: MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ADD of 1 row (hvx_mv_2d_repacked_q8_0), of 2 to 4 rows
(the multi-row path and the row-pair path), and of more rows without the HMX, MUL_MAT_NX
(hvx_mm_nx_2d_repacked_q8_0), and the four MUL_MAT_ID jobs, which get the pitch of the weight type
from hvx_mm_tiled_weight_pitch. The flat dots keep the pitch of 1152 bytes. The VTCM layout does not
change: a slot keeps n_k * 1152 bytes, and a dot reads at most 64 bytes after the last tile of its
slot. Q4_1, Q4_K and Q6_K have a tile size that is a multiple of 128 bytes: they get the same bytes
in VTCM through one descriptor row for each column tile. Q4_0 keeps its layout.

htp-opnode.h gives the kernel HTP_MM_KERNEL_HVX_QUANT_MULTIROW the name hvx-multirow in the profile
lines. It printed "unknown".

The results on the host

The kernel lab (tools/htp-lab, the release flags of the DSP library):
  - target q8: the 32x1, 32x2 and multi-row dots with the tiles in the VTCM layout of the tree and
    random bytes in the parts of each slot that hold no tile. On v79, for k = 96, 2080, 2560, 4096
    and 9216, each output hash of the patched tree is the hash of the checkout, and no output
    differs from the scalar reference. On v73, v75 and v81, for k = 2080 and 2560, each output hash
    of the patched tree is the hash of the checkout.
  - target gemvop (v79): op_matmul, op_matmul_nx and op_matmul_id with the kernel params of the
    host and a DMA shim that copies the rows of each descriptor: 25 cases (1 to 7 rows, the fused
    ADD, 2 and 3 weights, the expert paths, 100 and 200 weight rows, 3 and 65 k-tiles). Each output
    hash of the patched tree is the hash of the checkout, and each output agrees with a float64
    reference. The checkout pushes 80 descriptor rows for each column tile of k = 2560, the patched
    tree one.
  - The simulated cycles for each 32x32 weight tile on one thread (v79), the checkout against the
    patch, for k = 2560 (k = 9216 in parentheses):
      the 32x1 dot:                   52.6 and 50.6 (52.2 and 50.2)
      the 32x2 dot:                   92.4 and 93.4 (92.1 and 93.1)
      the multi-row dot of 1 row:     48.7 and 50.7 (48.2 and 50.2)
      the multi-row dot of 2 rows:    82.5 and 63.2 (82.1 and 62.3)
      the multi-row dot of 3 rows:    82.7 and 90.7 (82.2 and 90.2)
      the multi-row dot of 4 rows:   103.2 and 107.2 (125.0 and 122.3)
    At 62 GB/s the DMA of 6 threads gives one thread a tile every 222 cycles.
  - The packets of the loops (v79): the 32x1 dot has 22 packets for each tile (the checkout 21),
    with 18 aligned vector loads, 18 permutes (9 valign and 9 vshuff, the checkout 8 vror and 9
    vshuff) and 8 vrmpy. The loops of the multi-row dots have 23, 29, 39 and 49 packets for each
    tile of 1 to 4 rows (the checkout 21, 34, 40 and 48), with one permute more than the checkout.
    No dot uses an unaligned load. In the 32x1 and 32x2 dots the HVX stalls (COPROC_BUSY) set the
    cycles, not the permutes or the loads.

If the weight matmuls of a decode token read at 62 GB/s, they use approximately 72 ms, 13 ms less
(a calculation, not a measurement).

The results on the phone

The phone stage gemv (tools/stages/gemv, HTP0 v79, thermal status 0, no charger) compared a DSP
library with the DSP library of the series before this patch. That library had the DMA change and
the 32x1 and 32x2 dots of this patch, and multi-row dots with two k-tiles in each loop step:
  - test-backend-ops test of that library: the 30 cases of the 4B Q8_0 shapes (1 to 4 rows;
    the kernel is hvx-tiled for 1 row and hvx-multirow for 2 to 4 rows), the 43 Q8_0 MUL_MAT cases,
    the 75 Q8_0 MUL_MAT_ID cases, and the 62 MUL_MAT cases of Q4_1, Q4_K and Q6_K pass.
  - test-backend-ops perf of the 4B Q8_0 shapes: for 1 row the rate of the weights increases from
    49.4 to 54.9 GB/s to 57.2 to 61.8 GB/s (+12.0% to +15.7%), and for 4 rows by 6.5% to 13.9%.
  - llama-bench of the 4B Q8_0 (-fa on, a Q8_0 K and V cache, 3 alternated rounds, the change is the
    median of the ratios of one round; each run had a change of the cpu7 cap only). A decode token
    uses the 32x1 dot, and a prompt uses the HMX kernel:
      tg32 at the depth 0:        10.33 and 11.49 t/s (+11.3%)
      tg32 at the depth 4096:     10.19 and 11.35 t/s (+12.0%)
      tg32 at the depth 16384:     9.26 and 10.11 t/s (+10.0%)
      pp512 at the depth 0:       903.5 and 904.0 t/s (+0.1%)
    At the depth 4096 a token uses 10.0 ms less.
The phone stage gemv-check (HTP0 v79, thermal status 0, no charger) compared the DSP library of
this patch with the DSP library of the series before this patch:
  - gemvcheck check: the 4B decode matmuls (MUL_MAT, MUL_MAT_ADD, MUL_MAT_NX and the head, 1 to 4
    rows, and two shapes with an odd k-tile count) with the weights in a weight buffer, thus with
    the repack of the model loader. Each output of the v79 library (56 outputs), and of the v75 and
    v73 libraries on the v79 DSP (52 outputs each), has the bits of the base library. Each output
    is finite, and its NMSE against the CPU backend is less than 1e-8.
  - gemvcheck perf (16 copies of a case in one graph, the median of 5 computes, one run of each
    library, no change of the caps). For 1 row the rate increases by 1.9% to 14.3%. For 4 rows,
    in GB/s:
      gate 2560 x 4096:           49.14 and 53.29 (+8.4%)
      qkv 2560 x 8192:            52.18 and 57.23 (+9.7%)
      kv 2560 x 1024:             36.37 and 35.82 (-1.5%)
      out 4096 x 2560 + ADD:      47.58 and 51.73 (+8.7%)
      down 9216 x 2560 + ADD:     50.21 and 50.39 (+0.4%)
      NX gate + up 2560 x 9216:   47.16 and 52.63 (+11.6%)
      NX q + v + k 2560 x 8192:   52.19 and 55.99 (+7.3%)
    The seven cases of 4 rows use 2839 us against 3061 us (-7.2%). The base library gave 37.18
    GB/s for kv at 4 rows in the stage gemv, thus a difference of 2% for this case is in the
    spread of one run.

